      "message": "feat(post): probe on a machine with an inverted X/Y axis (Fanuc)\n\nThe engine's inverted-axis translation (post::invert) now mirrors the\nprobing records too, for a script that declares the new manifest flag\nprobeAxisInversion = true: a TOUCH's target and expected trip, a\nRETREAT's incremental delta and nominal end, and each FOUND axis's\nexpected value negate on the inverted axis. The script's trip read\n(#5061..), deviation (found - expected) and G10 L2 write then run in the\ncontroller's frame, where the work offset holds the part datum negated\non that axis, so the correction written is the physical one.\n\nOnly fanuc.cps declares the flag. Haas (motion verified, probing hooks\nstill in draft PR #360) refuses probing on an inverted axis by name;\nevery script without axisInversion keeps refusing the whole machine.\n\nProved by the new inverted_probe suite: a test-only Fanuc macro B\ncontroller (tests/macro_b: G0/G1 swept for collisions, G31 tripping on a\nsimulated part, G90/G91, G54..G59 registers, G10 L2, # arithmetic with\nbrackets/ABS/unary minus, IF..THEN/GOTO, #3000/#3006) runs the posted\nface/edge/corner/bore/boss programs on standard, inverted-X, -Y and -XY\nmachines with the part shifted off the model: G55 ends at the physical\ndatum in controller coordinates. A part out of reach alarms no contact;\none past tolerance alarms; the standard-sign program and a translation\nmissing any one probing word fail. Reads both #377 deviation forms.\n\nRefs #392\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
